
✅ What is Keycloak? (IAM / SSO / Auth Server)

Keycloak = open-source Identity & Access Management (IAM) server.
It is your central Auth Server / Identity Provider (IdP).

Break the terms:

  IAM (Identity & Access Management)
    → Who are you? (identity)
    → What are you allowed to do? (access / roles)

  Auth Server
    → Handles login, issues tokens, validates identity centrally
    → Apps trust Keycloak instead of each app storing passwords

  SSO (Single Sign-On)
    → Login once → use multiple apps without logging in again
    → Example: login to Salam portal → other Salam services accept same session/token

Keycloak typically provides:
  - Login / logout / password reset / MFA
  - User & role management
  - OAuth 2.0 + OpenID Connect (OIDC)
  - Token issuance (usually JWT access tokens)
  - SSO across clients in the same realm


═══════════════════════════════════════
1) Realm / Client / Roles (basics)
═══════════════════════════════════════

Realm
  → Isolated auth “world” (users, roles, clients live inside it)
  → Example: salam-dev, salam-prod  (or one realm “salam”)
  → Users in realm A cannot login to realm B

Client
  → An application registered inside the realm
  → Examples in Salam-style setup:
       - salam-web / frontend
       - salam-api-gateway
       - payment-service, user-service (resource servers)
  → Client settings: client-id, secret (if confidential), redirect URIs, roles

User
  → Person/account in the realm
  → Can live in Keycloak DB, OR come from external directory via federation (LDAP/AD)

What is LDAP / AD? (samajhne ke liye)
  LDAP = Lightweight Directory Access Protocol
         → standard way to look up users/groups in a directory
           (username, email, org unit, etc.)

  AD   = Active Directory (Microsoft)
         → company employee directory (very common in enterprises)
         → apps often say “LDAP/AD” together

  Default Keycloak:
    users stored in Keycloak’s own DB

  With LDAP/AD federation:
    Keycloak does NOT need every employee re-created manually
    → login is checked against company directory
    → Keycloak still issues tokens (JWT) after successful auth

  So “or LDAP/AD” means:
    identity source can be Keycloak itself OR company LDAP/Active Directory

Roles
  → Used for authorization after authentication

  Realm roles
    → Global in that realm (e.g. ADMIN, USER, AGENT)

  Client roles
    → Specific to one client/app (e.g. payment-service → PAYMENT_READ)

In token (JWT claims), roles often appear like:
  realm_access.roles
  resource_access.<client>.roles

Spring Security maps these to authorities (e.g. ROLE_ADMIN).


