4) Interview cheat sheet
═══════════════════════════════════════

Q: What is Keycloak?
A: Open-source IAM / auth server for login, users, roles, OAuth2/OIDC, SSO; issues tokens.

Q: How does login work with APIs?
A: User authenticates at Keycloak → gets JWT access token → APIs validate Bearer token via JWKS.

Q: Realm vs Client vs Role?
A: Realm = tenant/space; Client = app; Role = permission label used for authorization.

Q: How was it used with Gateway in Salam?
A: Keycloak issued tokens; API Gateway was entry point and validated/routed JWT requests;
   Spring Boot microservices acted as OAuth2 resource servers using Keycloak issuer/JWKS
   and enforced roles with Spring Security.


✍️ Sample Interview Answer (Salam-oriented)
"In Salam we used Keycloak as the central IAM/SSO server. Users logged in through Keycloak,
which issued JWT access tokens under our realm. Clients called APIs through the API Gateway
with Authorization Bearer tokens. The gateway routed traffic, and Spring Boot services were
configured as OAuth2 resource servers against the Keycloak issuer to validate JWT signature,
expiry, and roles before hitting business logic."


🧠 Interview one-liner
“Keycloak is the IAM/SSO auth server; it issues JWTs that the API Gateway and Spring Security resource servers validate for Salam APIs.”


